(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-mycobacterial-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Mycobacterial Infections and Tuberculosis Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which organism belongs to the Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex rather than to the nontuberculous mycobacteria?","choices":["Mycobacterium abscessus","Mycobacterium bovis","Mycobacterium avium complex","Mycobacterium kansasii"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex causes tuberculosis. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is its principal human pathogen, and the other members named are Mycobacterium bovis and Mycobacterium africanum. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which finding places a person in the 5 mm tuberculin skin test induration group?","choices":["An increased risk of exposure","No known tuberculosis risk factors","Imaging suggesting old tuberculosis","Work in a congregate care setting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The 5 mm group is human immunodeficiency virus infection, recent close contact, transplant, major immunosuppression, or imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. These people get the lowest cut-off because their risk is highest. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which situation can cause a false-negative tuberculin skin test?","choices":["Prior treated tuberculosis","Immunosuppression","BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccination","Nontuberculous mycobacterial exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immunosuppression or severe tuberculosis may cause false negatives, and early infection can also give a negative result. That is why symptoms or high clinical suspicion require disease evaluation now, whatever the skin test shows. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 23, 21, 25"},{"q":"In tuberculosis, what does massive hematogenous dissemination produce?","choices":["Miliary tuberculosis","A healed scar","Latent lesions","The primary complex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Massive hematogenous dissemination, from progressive primary or progressive secondary disease, produces miliary tuberculosis, with tiny lesions seeded through organs such as the liver and spleen. On imaging, miliary disease can produce diffuse tiny nodules. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 12, 27"},{"q":"In the standard regimen for drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis, which drugs continue for the final 4 months?","choices":["Rifampin and ethambutol","Isoniazid and pyrazinamide","Pyrazinamide and ethambutol","Isoniazid and rifampin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After 2 months of isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, isoniazid and rifampin continue for 4 more months, for 6 months in all. Daily therapy with directly observed therapy, in person or by video, supports completion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which regimen treats macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease?","choices":["Inhaled amikacin from the start","Azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin","Isoniazid, rifampin and pyrazinamide","Isoniazid, rifapentine, moxifloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease is treated with azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin, under specialist direction, for at least 12 months after culture conversion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"At what point in tuberculosis treatment is the response documented?","choices":["1 month","2 months","4 months","6 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sputum cultures are followed until conversion and the response is documented at 2 months, the end of the initial phase. Adherence, symptoms and adverse effects are reviewed at least monthly throughout. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the chest radiograph usually show in latent tuberculosis infection?","choices":["Diffuse tiny nodules","A normal film","Upper-lobe cavitation","Hilar adenopathy and effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Latent tuberculosis infection usually has a normal chest radiograph. By definition there is no evidence of disease after clinical evaluation, which includes chest imaging after a positive skin test or blood assay. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 10"},{"q":"Which chest film pattern is typical of post-primary tuberculosis?","choices":["A normal chest film","Diffuse tiny nodules","Upper-lobe disease that may cavitate","Hilar adenopathy with an effusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Post-primary disease often involves the upper lobes and may cavitate. Primary disease may show hilar or mediastinal adenopathy, infiltrates or effusion, and the patterns overlap, so the film cannot time the infection. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Besides compatible symptoms, compatible imaging and microbiology, which domain is required to diagnose nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease?","choices":["Cavitation on a plain chest film","Household exposure to a case","Exclusion of alternative diagnoses","Positive skin test and blood assay"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging, exclusion of alternative diagnoses, and the microbiologic criterion, usually 2 or more sputum cultures of the same species. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Who is eligible for the 17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen?","choices":["Age 12 or older with susceptible disease","Any age with rifampin resistance","Children under 5 with severe disease","Adults with latent infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen is for patients aged 12 or older with drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis. Eligibility, interactions and disease site require expert review. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"After a positive tuberculin skin test, what separates latent infection from tuberculosis disease?","choices":["The size of the induration","A follow-up blood assay","A repeat skin test in 8 weeks","Clinical evaluation and imaging"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neither the skin test nor the interferon-gamma release assay distinguishes latent infection from disease, so a positive result requires clinical evaluation and chest imaging; symptoms or suspicious imaging then require microbiologic testing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 22, 23"},{"q":"Which check is listed before choosing a latent tuberculosis infection regimen?","choices":["Sputum culture conversion","Monthly induration size","The 2-month response","Source-case drug resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Before a latent regimen is chosen, drug interactions, pregnancy considerations and source-case resistance are checked, and tuberculosis disease is excluded. A source case's resistance can make a regimen ineffective. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How far apart are the sputum specimens for suspected pulmonary tuberculosis collected?","choices":["1-2 hours","4-6 hours","8-24 hours","48-72 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The three sputum specimens are collected 8 to 24 hours apart, with at least one early-morning specimen, and sent for acid-fast smear, mycobacterial culture and, on at least one, a rapid molecular test. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"When is the local or state tuberculosis program notified?","choices":["When rifampin resistance is found","For presumed or confirmed disease","After culture confirmation arrives","After treatment is completed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immediate management of pulmonary tuberculosis starts with notifying the local or state tuberculosis program of presumed or confirmed disease, alongside airborne isolation when indicated and fit-tested N95 or higher respiratory protection for staff. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"After a recent tuberculosis exposure, a first tuberculin skin test is negative. When is the test repeated?","choices":["2-4 weeks after the last exposure","4-6 weeks after the last exposure","8-10 weeks after the last exposure","16-20 weeks after the last exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test or blood assay, so testing is repeated 8 to 10 weeks after the last exposure when indicated. Symptoms or high clinical suspicion still require disease evaluation now. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which endemic fungal infection is named on the differential for pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Pneumocystis pneumonia","Nontuberculous mycobacteria","Candidiasis","Histoplasmosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Endemic fungal infection, including histoplasmosis, is on the pulmonary tuberculosis differential, with nontuberculous mycobacterial disease, bacterial pneumonia or lung abscess, bronchiectasis or chronic pulmonary aspergillosis, and lung cancer. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"During evaluation of suspected pulmonary tuberculosis, which test is offered when the patient's status for a coexisting infection that raises progression risk is unknown?","choices":["Repeat skin test in 8 weeks","Routine chest computed tomography","Interferon-gamma release assay","Human immunodeficiency virus test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The microbiologic evaluation of suspected pulmonary tuberculosis includes offering human immunodeficiency virus testing when status is unknown, alongside sputum smear, culture, molecular and drug-susceptibility testing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which respiratory protection do staff use when caring for a patient with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Patient source control by itself","Gloves and gown by themselves","Fit-tested N95 or higher","No mask once therapy has begun"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staff use fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection, the patient is placed in an airborne infection isolation room when indicated, and the patient uses source control during transport. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 33, 13"},{"q":"How are most nontuberculous mycobacterial infections acquired?","choices":["Reactivation of a latent focus","Reinfection from a household case","Environmental exposure","Airborne spread from a case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most nontuberculous mycobacterial infections arise from environmental exposure, and person-to-person spread is uncommon. These are environmental mycobacteria with both slow and rapid growers. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which description fits Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","choices":["Principal human pathogen of its complex","Rapid-growing environmental species","Acquired mainly from soil and water","Organism with no detectable cell wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the principal human pathogen of the Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex, the group that causes tuberculosis. It is a slow-growing acid-fast bacillus whose cultures can take weeks. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 5"},{"q":"What does a normal physical examination mean in suspected pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["It excludes pulmonary disease","It points to latent infection","It means the patient is not infectious","It does not exclude the disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Physical examination has limited sensitivity in pulmonary tuberculosis. Findings may include crackles or bronchial breath sounds, and fever, wasting or lymphadenopathy can give clues, but a normal examination does not exclude the disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which history is an exposure risk for tuberculosis rather than a progression risk?","choices":["Diabetes mellitus","Prolonged corticosteroid therapy","Chronic kidney disease on dialysis","Travel to a high-incidence area"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exposure risks are close contact with infectious tuberculosis, prolonged exposure in enclosed, poorly ventilated spaces, residence or work in settings with ongoing transmission, and travel or residence in areas with high incidence. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 14, 15"},{"q":"Which contacts of an infectious tuberculosis case need prompt public-health assessment?","choices":["Young children and the immunocompromised","Adult contacts with a negative first test","Contacts with prior vaccination scars","Adult contacts who have no symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Young children and immunocompromised contacts need prompt public-health assessment, because both carry a higher risk of progression, and early infection or immunosuppression can make the first skin test or blood assay falsely negative. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 23, 15"},{"q":"How long is the shortened regimen for selected children with nonsevere drug-susceptible tuberculosis?","choices":["4 months","6 months","9 months","12 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selected children with nonsevere tuberculosis can receive 4 months of treatment: 2 months of isoniazid, rifampin and pyrazinamide, with or without ethambutol, then 2 months of isoniazid and rifampin. Eligibility requires expert review. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What supports completion of daily tuberculosis therapy?","choices":["Directly observed therapy","Self-reported dosing diaries","Monthly sputum smears","Twice-yearly clinic visits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Daily therapy with directly observed therapy, in person or by video, supports completion of the standard regimen. Adherence, symptoms and adverse effects are reviewed at least monthly. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why is an interferon-gamma release assay preferred over the tuberculin skin test for most BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin)-vaccinated patients?","choices":["The vaccine makes skin tests falsely negative","It separates latent from active disease","Its cut-offs are adjusted for vaccine history","It is not made positive by the vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccination does not cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay, which measures immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens, whereas the vaccine can cause a false-positive skin test. The assay is therefore preferred for most vaccinated patients. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 24, 21, 19"},{"q":"How is the response to tuberculosis treatment monitored?","choices":["By repeat blood assays until negative","By serial chest films alone","By clinical and microbiologic response","By repeat skin tests until negative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The skin test and the interferon-gamma release assay may stay positive after treatment, so therapy is monitored by clinical and microbiologic response, following sputum cultures until conversion and documenting response at 2 months. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 36"},{"q":"Which finding is typical of tuberculous lymph node involvement?","choices":["Node enlargement with back pain","Persistent, often painless enlargement","Tender node that resolves in days","Node enlargement with pleuritic pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Extrapulmonary tuberculosis can involve almost any organ. Lymph node disease shows persistent, often painless enlargement, and organ-specific specimens and urgent assessment may be needed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which tests are requested on the sputum specimens in suspected pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Acid-fast smear and culture","Interferon-gamma release assay","Tuberculin skin test reading","Serum antigen and antibody"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each specimen is sent for an acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one respiratory specimen and molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
